            "new allocates on the heap; you must delete it yourself:\n\nint* p = new int(42);   delete p;\nint* arr = new int[n];  delete[] arr;   // [] for arrays!\n\nForgetting delete leaks memory; deleting twice is undefined behaviour. In modern C++ you rarely write new/delete - use std::vector, std::string, or smart pointers (Unit 14) instead. This is RAII: resources are tied to object lifetimes.",
